{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305205,"nonce":"CiJzMBjBMOrFr4p4xpTtj9EiF/s=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiHAA","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/22","origin":"github"},"title":"Candidate files are opened, seeked, read and closed once per piece","message":"```python\ndef check_file_chunk(hash, offset, length, filename):\n    with open(filename, \"rb\") as sfile:\n        sfile.seek(offset)\n        piece = sfile.read(length)\n```\n\nThis runs per piece. A 1 TB torrent at 1 MiB pieces performs on the order of a million `open`/`close` pairs on the same handful of files.\n\nBeyond the syscall and path-resolution cost, each `open` creates a fresh buffered reader, so the kernel gets no continuity and sequential readahead never engages — a sequential scan is issued as a series of unrelated positioned reads.\n\nA bounded LRU cache of open file descriptors, reused across pieces, fixes this. Respecting `ulimit -n` matters for torrents with many files.","files":null}]}